# Line endings, decided by the repository rather than by whoever cloned it.
#
# Without this, git shows every file as modified the moment an editor on
# Windows rewrites it with CRLF - 109 files at once, none of them
# containing a single changed character. A diff that large is not read,
# and a real change hidden inside it is not seen either.
#
# LF everywhere, including in the working tree, because everything here
# runs on Linux: the application in its containers, the test suite in CI.
# A Windows checkout that produced CRLF would be writing files for a
# platform this project never executes on.
#
# Applying this to an existing clone needs one command, once:
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#     git commit -m "Normalise line endings"
#
# After that the question does not come up again.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary. Never converted, never diffed as text - a "corrected" line
# ending inside a PNG is a corrupted PNG.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary
*.pptx binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
